    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "848ff2ac_1ea92d5f",
        "filename": "docs/html/training/articles/smp.jd",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 123,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081378
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-09-11T21:16:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The phrasing in terms of \"processors\", and similarly below in terms of \"CPU architectures\", is troublesome. Programmers should normally be thinking in terms of the *platform* (whose abstraction boundary is the compiler), not the CPU. It\u0027s OK for this section since it\u0027s fairly CPU-centric, but as you state below, the concept of \"sequential consistency\" is the only thing that really matters beyond this section, so it\u0027d be better if it were phrased at the level of abstraction people are programming on.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 31,
        "endLine": 123,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "00e2d2be4a60d6fc010d531dfe48781574fd9428",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "84a1b20e_d1da2602",
        "filename": "docs/html/training/articles/smp.jd",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 127,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081378
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-09-11T21:16:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe a better way to phrase this would be to say that the litmus tests below are written in the assembly language of our hypothetical CPU?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 127,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 127,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "00e2d2be4a60d6fc010d531dfe48781574fd9428",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e467cecb_d906f35b",
